    },
    {
      "text": "2.2.1",
      "bbox": [
        133.76797485351562,
        238.4059600830078,
        157.3096160888672,
        247.25274658203125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Trace containment",
      "bbox": [
        168.7666015625,
        238.4059600830078,
        261.9667053222656,
        247.25274658203125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "We will develop reasoning techniques based on reachability, abstraction, com-",
      "bbox": [
        133.76797485351562,
        256.0779724121094,
        477.50762939453125,
        264.92474365234375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "position, and substitutivity. To this end, we will need to establish containment",
      "bbox": [
        133.76797485351562,
        267.3159484863281,
        477.527587890625,
        276.1627197265625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "relations between the behaviors of systems. Here we deﬁne containment of tran-",
      "bbox": [
        133.76797485351562,
        278.553955078125,
        477.4877014160156,
        287.4007263183594
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "sition graph traces. Consider transition graphs G1, G2, with modes L1, L2, and a",
      "bbox": [
        133.76797485351562,
        289.7919616699219,
        477.4856262207031,
        299.5528869628906
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "mode map lmap : L1 → L2. For a trace σ = ℓ1, t1, ℓ2, t2, . . . , ℓk ∈TraceG1, sim-",
      "bbox": [
        133.7679443359375,
        300.4720458984375,
        477.4759216308594,
        311.4003601074219
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "plifying notation, we denote by lmap(σ) the sequence lmap(ℓ1), t1, lmap(ℓ2), t2,",
      "bbox": [
        133.76791381835938,
        312.2679748535156,
        477.48150634765625,
        322.0289001464844
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": ". . . , lmap(ℓk). We write G1 ⪯lmap G2 iﬀfor every trace σ ∈TraceG1, there is a",
      "bbox": [
        133.76791381835938,
        322.94805908203125,
        477.4797058105469,
        333.8763732910156
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "trace σ′ ∈TraceG2 such that lmap(σ) is a preﬁx of σ′.",
      "bbox": [
        133.7679443359375,
        330.570068359375,
        369.26251220703125,
        346.08074951171875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Deﬁnition 2.2. Given graphs G1, G2 and a mode map lmap : L1 → L2, a",
      "bbox": [
        133.76791381835938,
        353.1780700683594,
        477.4849853515625,
        364.0767517089844
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "relation R ⊆V1 × V2 is a forward simulation relation from G1 to G2 iﬀ",
      "bbox": [
        133.76791381835938,
        364.41607666015625,
        443.6267395019531,
        375.31475830078125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "(a) for each v ∈V1init, there is u ∈V2init such that (v, u) ∈R,",
      "bbox": [
        133.7679443359375,
        383.4080810546875,
        400.23553466796875,
        394.3077697753906
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "(b) for every (v, u) ∈R, lmap(vlab1(v)) = vlab2(u), and",
      "bbox": [
        133.7679443359375,
        398.57708740234375,
        379.0662536621094,
        408.8969421386719
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "(c) for every (v, v′) ∈E1 and (v, u) ∈R, there exists a ﬁnite set u1, . . . , uk such",
      "bbox": [
        134.3219451904297,
        412.3727111816406,
        477.47705078125,
        424.6457824707031
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "that: (i) for each uj, (v, uj) ∈R, and (ii) elab1((v, v′)) ⊆∪jelab2((u, uj)).",
      "bbox": [
        151.47991943359375,
        423.6107177734375,
        472.732421875,
        435.303955078125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Proposition 2.3. If there exists a forward simulation relation from G1 to G2",
      "bbox": [
        133.76791381835938,
        444.5350341796875,
        476.9850158691406,
        454.87579345703125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "with lmap then G1 ⪯lmap G2.",
      "bbox": [
        133.7679443359375,
        455.215087890625,
        262.82147216796875,
        466.11376953125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "2.2.2",
      "bbox": [
        133.7679443359375,
        478.697021484375,
        157.30958557128906,
        487.5437927246094
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Sequential composition of graphs",
      "bbox": [
        168.76657104492188,
        478.697021484375,
        334.6338195800781,
        487.5437927246094
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "We will ﬁnd it convenient to deﬁne the sequential composition of two transition",
      "bbox": [
        133.7679443359375,
        496.3690185546875,
        477.4981689453125,
        505.2157897949219
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "graphs. Intuitively, the traces of the composition of G1 and G2 will be those that",
      "bbox": [
        133.76791381835938,
        507.6070251464844,
        477.48785400390625,
        517.94775390625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "can be obtained by concatenating a trace of G1 with a trace of G2. To keep",
      "bbox": [
        133.7679443359375,
        518.844970703125,
        477.4761962890625,
        529.185791015625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "the deﬁnitions and notations simple, we will assume (when taking sequential",
      "bbox": [
        133.76797485351562,
        530.0830078125,
        477.5075378417969,
        538.9298095703125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "compositions) |Vinit| = |Vterm| = 1; this is true of the examples we analyze. It is",
      "bbox": [
        133.76797485351562,
        540.76318359375,
        477.4813232421875,
        551.0819702148438
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "easy to generalize to the case when this does not hold. Under this assumption,",
      "bbox": [
        133.76795959472656,
        552.5590209960938,
        477.49774169921875,
        561.4058227539062
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "the unique vertex in Vinit will be denoted as vinit and the unique vertex in Vterm",
      "bbox": [
        133.76795959472656,
        563.2381591796875,
        476.98394775390625,
        574.1378173828125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "will be denoted as vterm.",
      "bbox": [
        133.76797485351562,
        575.0339965820312,
        238.49559020996094,
        584.7959594726562
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Deﬁnition 2.4. Given graphs G1 = ⟨ L, V1, E1, vlab1, elab1⟩and G2 = ⟨ L, V2, E2,",
      "bbox": [
        133.76797485351562,
        593.4691162109375,
        477.48162841796875,
        604.3677978515625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "vlab2, elab2⟩such that vlab1(v1term) = vlab2(v2init), the sequential composition",
      "bbox": [
        133.76803588867188,
        604.7071533203125,
        477.4862976074219,
        615.0260009765625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "of G1 and G2 is the graph G1 ◦G2 = ⟨ L, V, E, vlab, elab⟩where",
      "bbox": [
        133.76803588867188,
        615.9451904296875,
        407.27337646484375,
        626.8438110351562
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "(a) V = (V1 ∪V2) \\ {v2init)},",
      "bbox": [
        133.76803588867188,
        634.9371337890625,
        257.005615234375,
        645.8367919921875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "(b) E = E1 ∪{(v1term, u) | (v2init, u) ∈E2} ∪{(v, u) ∈E2 | v̸ = v2init},",
      "bbox": [
        133.76800537109375,
        650.106201171875,
        423.87457275390625,
        661.005859375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "(c) vlab(v) = vlab1(v) if v ∈V1 and vlab(v) = vlab2(v) if v ∈V2,",
      "bbox": [
        134.32199096679688,
        665.275146484375,
        416.5576171875,
        676.1748046875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
